What is a prototype-based language?
“A language where objects inherit from other objects, not from classes.”
 Why is JavaScript prototype-based?
Because:
In JavaScript, every object has a hidden parent object, called prototype.


When you call a method, JavaScript searches in:


That object


Its prototype


Then prototype’s prototype
 (This chain is called prototype chain)


Even when you write:
class User {}
JavaScript secretly converts it to:
function User() {}
User.prototype = { ... }

So class keyword is fake → it's a clean syntax, but internally JavaScript is still prototype based.

 Interview answer (easy English)
Here is the best answer you can tell in interviews:
“JavaScript is not class-based like Java.
 JavaScript uses prototypes — objects inherit from other objects.
 The class keyword in JavaScript is only a cleaner syntax, but behind the scenes JavaScript still uses prototypes.
 So we say JavaScript is a prototype-based language.”
class, prototype, and object creation in JavaScript step by step
=====================================================
Step 1: 
You might write:
class User {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  sayHi() {
    console.log(`Hi, I am ${this.name}`);
  }
}

You see class → you think it’s like Java class.


Reality: JS does not create a real class.


It only creates a function + a prototype object behind the scenes.



⭐ Step 2 — Internally, JS converts class to function + prototype
Your class:
class User {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  sayHi() { console.log(`Hi, I am ${this.name}`); }
}

Becomes internally:
function User(name) {
  this.name = name;  // this will be the object properties
}

User.prototype.sayHi = function() {
  console.log(`Hi, I am ${this.name}`);
}

Explanation in simple terms:
The constructor becomes a normal function User().


The methods inside the class (sayHi) go to User.prototype.


Every object created from User links to this prototype.



⭐ Step 3 — What happens when we use new
const user1 = new User("Ajmal");

When JS sees new User("Ajmal"), it does 4 things:
Create a new empty object

 user1 = {}


Set its prototype to User.prototype

 user1.__proto__ = User.prototype
 → Now user1 can use all methods in User.prototype like sayHi()


Run the constructor function with this pointing to user1

 this.name = "Ajmal"
 → Now user1 has property name = "Ajmal"


Return the object automatically

 return user1



⭐ Step 4 — Where the method is stored
user1.sayHi();  // Hi, I am Ajmal
sayHi() is not inside user1 itself.


JavaScript looks at user1.__proto__ (User.prototype) → finds sayHi() → executes it.


✅ This is prototype-based inheritance.

